<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>What appraisal clause representation means</h2>
<p>Nearly every California property policy contains an appraisal provision. The baseline wording comes from the standard fire policy in California Insurance Code §2071: if you and the insurer fail to agree on the actual cash value or the amount of loss, either side may demand appraisal in writing. Each party then selects a competent and disinterested appraiser, the two appraisers pick an umpire, and an itemized written award signed by any two of the three sets the amount.</p>
<p>Appraisal clause representation means we are the appraiser you select. We inspect the property, build an independent line-item estimate, meet with the carrier’s appraiser, and present any unresolved items to the umpire. We do not argue coverage, and we do not give legal advice. Our job is the number.</p>
<h2>Deadlines inside the clause</h2>
<p>The §2071 form gives each side 20 days after a written demand to name its appraiser. If the two appraisers can’t agree on an umpire within 15 days, either party can ask a judge of a court of record in the county where the property sits to appoint one. Your own policy may track this language or add to it, so we read the actual form before anything is sent.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Before the demand:</strong> we review the policy, the carrier’s estimate and your photos, and tell you whether the gap is large enough to justify appraisal.</li>
<li><strong>The demand letter:</strong> it should identify the claim, the items in dispute and your appraiser. Many owners send it themselves; some have counsel send it.</li>
<li><strong>After the demand:</strong> we contact the carrier’s appraiser, agree on an umpire and set an inspection schedule.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How California keeps appraisal informal</h2>
<p>The §2071 language describes appraisal as informal unless both sides agree otherwise. In practice that means no depositions, no interrogatories and no court reporter. The panel works from inspections, estimates, photos, invoices and expert reports. That is why the quality of the written file matters so much: there is no cross-examination to fix a weak estimate later.</p>
<h3>What we prepare for the panel</h3>
<ul>
<li>A room-by-room or slope-by-slope scope with measurements and photos tied to each line.</li>
<li>Pricing that reflects the local market for the property’s ZIP code, with notes where labor or material costs moved after the loss.</li>
<li>A side-by-side comparison against the carrier’s estimate, so the umpire can see exactly where the two numbers part ways.</li>
<li>Separate actual cash value and replacement cost columns, because the award should state both for each item.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What the panel can and cannot decide</h2>
<p>California courts have generally held that appraisers decide value, not coverage, causation or the meaning of the policy. If the carrier says part of the damage isn’t covered, that disagreement usually needs to be resolved separately, sometimes by an attorney. We will flag those issues early so you aren’t surprised after the award is signed.</p>
<h2>Our independence</h2>
<p>The policy requires each appraiser to be disinterested. We disclose prior relationships with the carrier, its appraiser and the proposed umpire, and we put our fee agreement in writing before we start. If we have a conflict, we say so and step aside.</p>

<h2>Questions about appraisal clause representation</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>Do I need a lawyer to invoke appraisal in California?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Not necessarily. Many owners send the written demand themselves. If coverage is disputed, a deadline is close, or the carrier refuses to participate, talking to a California attorney first is wise.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can the insurer refuse appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>A carrier may argue the dispute is about coverage rather than value. When that happens, the disagreement may have to be sorted out before or alongside appraisal. We will tell you if we see that risk.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays for the umpire?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Under the §2071 form, each side pays its own appraiser and the umpire’s fee and other appraisal expenses are split equally.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the award final?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>An itemized award signed by any two of the three panel members generally sets the amount of loss. It can be challenged only on narrow grounds, so the file should be complete before the award is signed.</p></div></details></div>
